  "maintainers": ["RadeenXALNW"],
  "units": "m",
  "description": "Newton's first law (নিউটনের প্রথম সূত্র, page 64, 3.1) as a thought experiment the student runs: a physics textbook waits on a launcher and one tap gives it the same push every time (it leaves at 3 m/s) onto the floor chosen in the panel: a jute mat, a wooden floor, ice, or an imagined floor with no friction. Without a push it stays at rest. With friction it slows evenly and stops (1.31 m, 2.55 m, 9.18 m; a post marks each stop and the chips keep the distances); with no friction the velocity readout stays at 3.00 m/s, the marks it leaves every 0.1 s stay evenly spaced, and the camera follows it for ever. Arrows show the velocity, friction against it, and the push only while the paddle touches the book. Test tab: 5 questions (what a pushed book does with no friction, why a book stops, predicting the ice or the mat from the other floors, which way a book goes when a curved wall ends, a spacecraft with its engines off or a book at rest on a frictionless floor). Phone-first, Bangla.",
  "tags": ["force", "newtons-laws", "inertia", "friction", "uniform-velocity", "bangla"],
